  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.200HsSeCmp0rIIhq3n1ivp",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"blk720c5dce00c0\"].children[_key==\"s164cb485a680\"].text": "For families with young kids, one thing about Disney World holds true: it is absolutely worth the logistical headache — but only if you go in with a real plan. Not the \"we'll figure it out\" kind of plan. The kind where you know which park you're walking into, what you're eating, and approximately how long you'll wait for Rise of the Resistance (because it will be long, every single time)."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-9b3c8e9ed1cc\"].children[_key==\"span-9b3c8e9ed1cc\"].text": "If you're searching for what to pack for a Disney World vacation with kids in 2026, this checklist is built for that. It covers 65+ items organized by category, updated for 2026 park policies, Lightning Lane requirements, and Orlando's typical weather by season."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-33a91940982d\"].children[_key==\"span-33a91940982d\"].text": "Orlando summers are hot and humid: average daily highs at Orlando International Airport are about 91–92°F in June through August, and winter (December–February) highs average about 72–75°F (NOAA NCEI 1991–2020 climate normals, Orlando International Airport, accessed Oct 16, 2026). Indoor attractions and restaurants are often heavily air-conditioned. Young kids can overheat quickly, so plan shade and water breaks and pack accordingly:"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-23658bb9bc8d\"].children[_key==\"s879be0cfbb33\"].text": "Summer Disney World packing with kids requires extra focus on heat management: SPF 50+ lotion sunscreen (not aerosol spray), cooling towels, cooling neck gaiters, electrolyte packets, insulated water bottles for each family member, and a USB rechargeable handheld fan. July and August average highs in Orlando are about 92°F, with high humidity (NOAA NCEI 1991–2020 climate normals, Orlando International Airport, accessed Oct 16, 2026). Plan regular shade and water breaks for young kids, and pack more cooling supplies than you think you need."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-df13f26c389e\"].children[_key==\"span-df13f26c389e\"].text": "Winter Disney World (December–February) average daytime highs are about 72–75°F, with average lows around 50–53°F (NOAA NCEI 1991–2020 climate normals, Orlando International Airport, accessed Oct 16, 2026), and indoor spaces are often heavily air-conditioned. Pack layers for everyone: a light jacket or fleece, moisture-wicking base layers, and a rain jacket for unexpected rain. Sunscreen is still worth packing — kids can burn even on cooler sunny days."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-d9d46275df0d\"].children[_key==\"span-d9d46275df0d\"].text": "If you only pack five extras, prioritize: a portable phone charger (20,000mAh+), comfortable broken-in walking shoes, a cooling towel, an insulated water bottle for each family member, and rain ponchos for the whole family. These cover the most common park-day problems: dead phones, sore feet, heat, thirst, and afternoon rain."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-af4834551c09\"].children[_key==\"span-af4834551c09\"].text": "Everything above is available on Amazon — delivered to your hotel or home before your trip. Use our packing list links above to grab what your family needs before you go. If you're short on time, start with a 20,000mAh charger, a cooling towel, packing cubes, and a good pair of walking shoes."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list-kids",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"86642c220973\"].children[_key==\"e531a32a974e\"].text": "This isn't a generic 100-item packing list. It's 25 practical items for Disney World with young kids, chosen for the heat, crowds, and long days of a typical park visit. It's organized by what matters most, with age-specific guidance for toddlers through early elementary."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list-kids",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"b39c434970ca\"].children[_key==\"93ba542451d8\"].text": "\nOrlando summer days are hot and humid, and young kids can't always tell you they're too hot. Wet a cooling towel, drape it on their neck, and pair it with shade and water breaks. If a child shows signs of heat illness, get help from park First Aid or a doctor."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list-kids",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"3617679e59a6\"].children[_key==\"3aee82b2d791\"].text": "\nFlorida heat + a napping toddler in a stroller = a very unhappy kid. A clip-on stroller fan (some have a misting option) moves air and can make a shaded stroller more comfortable. Follow the maker's safety guidance, keep fingers and straps clear, and don't rely on a fan alone in high heat. USB rechargeable means you top it off from a battery pack during lunch."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list-kids",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"c5ea5147885d\"].children[_key==\"9318dabb0d4f\"].text": "\nDisney provides free water at quick-service restaurants. Bring a bottle and refill it often — regular water breaks are one of the simplest ways to keep a long park day going."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list-kids",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"ed060f2081c0\"].children[_key==\"0e47173321ee\"].text": "\nFrozen water bottles count as a liquid, but they'll thaw by the time you land — giving you ice-cold water in the Orlando heat. Swap for a refill at the airport or hotel so everyone starts the trip with water on hand."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-0027d4574016\"].children[_key==\"se90f50839437\"].text": "\nOn hot days with active kids, some families add electrolyte drink packets to water bottles. Check labels and ask your pediatrician if you're unsure what's right for your child's age. If a child shows signs of heat illness, get help from park First Aid or a doctor."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-a3aa6cec2ca7\"].children[_key==\"s631502cc3438\"].text": "\nTravel days and new food can be rough on little stomachs; pack whatever your pediatrician recommends for your child. If a child seems unwell, get help from park First Aid or a doctor."
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "patch": {
    "id": "drafts.disney-world-packing-list",
    "set": {
     "body[_key==\"block-22cdf86bac9a\"].children[_key==\"span-22cdf86bac9a\"].text": "Park days are physically demanding for kids. Every Disney park has a First Aid station, but it may be a long walk away, so a small family kit covers the everyday basics like bandages and blister care:"
    }
   }
  }
 ]
}